export const C6={title:'Immunology, serology and blood banking',hook:'Before a unit of blood can be transfused, the laboratory must prove that the patient’s antibodies will not attack the donor’s red cells. A mistake can cause a fatal transfusion reaction. Immunology—the largest section of the CLS test plan—explains how antigens and antibodies react, and HOSA’s ABO grouping skill puts that knowledge on a slide.',objectives:['Describe innate and adaptive immunity, antigens and antibodies.','Explain serologic test principles: agglutination, precipitation, ELISA and rapid immunoassays.','Determine ABO and Rh type from forward typing and perform HOSA Skill IV.','Apply transfusion compatibility rules and explain hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn.'],sections:[
['Immune system foundations',[
'**Innate immunity** is the body’s fast, nonspecific defense: skin and mucous membranes, phagocytes such as neutrophils and macrophages, natural killer cells, complement proteins, inflammation and fever. **Adaptive immunity** is specific and has memory. **B lymphocytes** mature into **plasma cells** that secrete **antibodies** (humoral immunity); **T lymphocytes** include **helper T cells (CD4)** that coordinate responses and **cytotoxic T cells (CD8)** that kill infected cells (cell-mediated immunity). **Antigens** are molecules that trigger responses. **Antibodies** (immunoglobulins) bind antigens: **IgG** is most abundant and crosses the placenta, **IgM** appears first in an infection and is an efficient agglutinator, **IgA** protects mucous membranes, **IgE** mediates allergy and **IgD** sits on B cells. **Active immunity** comes from infection or vaccination; **passive immunity** comes from transferred antibodies, as from mother to fetus.',
match('Immunology terms',[['Fast, nonspecific defenses','Innate immunity'],['Cells that secrete antibodies','Plasma cells'],['Coordinates the immune response (CD4)','Helper T cell'],['First antibody in a new infection','IgM'],['Crosses the placenta','IgG'],['Antibodies received from another source','Passive immunity']])]],
['Serologic testing',[
'**Serology** detects antibodies or antigens in serum. **Agglutination** tests use particles—red cells or latex beads coated with antigen or antibody—that clump visibly when the matching partner is present; the **rapid latex agglutination D-dimer test** and blood typing are examples. **Precipitation** tests detect soluble antigen–antibody complexes. **ELISA** uses enzyme-linked antibodies and a color change and can be quantitative. **Lateral flow chromatographic immunoassays**, such as the **urine hCG pregnancy test** on HOSA’s list, move sample along a strip where labeled antibodies capture the target and form a line; a **control line** must appear for the test to be valid. A **titer** is the highest dilution still positive and indicates antibody quantity. Rising titers between acute and convalescent samples suggest recent infection.']],
['ABO and Rh blood groups',[
'Red cells carry inherited **antigens**. In the **ABO system**, type A cells have **A antigen** and the plasma contains **anti-B antibodies**; type B cells have B antigen and anti-A antibodies; type AB cells have both antigens and **neither antibody**; type O cells have **neither antigen** and **both anti-A and anti-B antibodies**. The **Rh (D) antigen** makes a person Rh-positive; Rh-negative people do not naturally have anti-D but can form it after exposure to Rh-positive blood. **Forward (cell) typing** mixes patient red cells with anti-A and anti-B (and anti-D) reagents—**agglutination means the antigen is present**. **Reverse (serum) typing** mixes patient plasma with known A and B cells to confirm the antibodies; both must agree.',
{t:'w',kind:'abotype',title:'Determine blood types from agglutination'},
sort('What antibodies are in the plasma?',['Anti-B only','Anti-A only','Both anti-A and anti-B','Neither'],[['Type A',0],['Type B',1],['Type O',2],['Type AB',3]])]],
['ABO slide grouping (HOSA Skill IV)',[
'HOSA Skill IV uses simulated blood on a two-well slide. After assembling materials, hand hygiene and gloves, the competitor **labels the slide with the patient’s name**, places **three drops of patient blood** in each of the A and B wells **without letting the dropper touch the slide**, adds **three drops of anti-A serum** to the A well and **three drops of anti-B serum** to the B well, and stirs each well with a **separate clean stirrer for 30 seconds**, using an effective motion that avoids splattering. They **record agglutination** on the ABO worksheet and determine the **blood type and transfusion responses** on the laboratory report form. Clean-up: disposable labware into the biohazard receptacle, return simulated blood and reagents to storage, remove gloves into biohazard and perform hand hygiene.',
order('ABO slide grouping (HOSA Skill IV)',['Assemble materials, perform hand hygiene and put on gloves','Label the slide with the patient’s name','Add three drops of blood to each well without touching the slide','Add three drops of anti-A to the A well and anti-B to the B well','Stir each well with a separate clean stirrer for 30 seconds','Record agglutination and determine the blood type','Determine compatible donor types on the report','Discard labware, store reagents, remove gloves and wash hands'])]],
['Transfusion compatibility and HDFN',[
'For **red cell transfusion**, the donor’s cells must lack antigens the recipient has antibodies against. **O-negative** red cells are the **universal red cell donor** type in emergencies; **AB-positive** patients are **universal red cell recipients**. For **plasma**, the rule reverses: **AB plasma** is the universal plasma donor because it contains no anti-A or anti-B. Before routine transfusion, the blood bank performs a **type and screen** (ABO/Rh and antibody screen) and a **crossmatch** between recipient plasma and donor cells. Units are stored in a monitored **blood bank refrigerator** (red cells at about 1–6 °C). An **acute hemolytic transfusion reaction**, usually from ABO incompatibility caused by misidentification, can cause fever, back pain, red urine and shock; the transfusion is stopped immediately.',
'**Hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn (HDFN)** occurs when an Rh-negative mother has formed anti-D from a previous Rh-positive pregnancy or transfusion, and her IgG crosses the placenta to destroy an Rh-positive fetus’s red cells. **Rh immune globulin (RhIG, such as RhoGAM)** given to Rh-negative mothers during pregnancy and after delivery of an Rh-positive baby prevents sensitization.',
kase('Choosing blood in an emergency',{a:{text:'A trauma patient is bleeding heavily, and there is no time to type the blood. Which red cells are safest to give immediately?',choices:[['O-negative red cells','b','Correct. They lack A, B and D antigens.',true],['AB-positive red cells','b','AB-positive cells carry A, B and D antigens and could be attacked.',false]]},b:{text:'Typing later shows the patient is A-positive. Which red cell types are compatible?',choices:[['A-positive, A-negative, O-positive and O-negative','c','Correct. The patient has anti-B, so cells must lack B antigen.',true],['B-positive','c','The patient’s anti-B would destroy B cells.',false]]},c:{text:'The blood bank switches to type-specific A units after crossmatching, conserving O-negative for patients who need it.',end:true}})]],
["Hypersensitivity, autoimmunity and immunodeficiency",["The immune system can overreact or misdirect. **Hypersensitivity** reactions are classified into four types. **Type I (immediate)** reactions are mediated by **IgE** and mast cells, as in hay fever, food allergy and **anaphylaxis**. **Type II (cytotoxic)** reactions involve antibodies against cell-surface antigens, as in **hemolytic transfusion reactions** and HDFN. **Type III (immune complex)** reactions deposit antigen–antibody complexes in tissues, as in some forms of glomerulonephritis and lupus. **Type IV (delayed)** reactions are T cell–mediated, as in poison ivy dermatitis and the **tuberculin skin test**, read 48–72 hours after placement.","**Autoimmune diseases** occur when the immune system attacks the body’s own tissues; laboratory tests detect **autoantibodies** such as **antinuclear antibodies (ANA)** in systemic lupus erythematosus and **rheumatoid factor** in rheumatoid arthritis. **Immunodeficiency** can be inherited or acquired; **HIV** infects CD4 helper T cells, and laboratories monitor **CD4 counts** and **viral load**. HIV screening uses antigen/antibody combination immunoassays, with positive results confirmed by additional testing."]]
],takeaways:['Adaptive immunity: B cells/plasma cells make antibodies; IgM first, IgG most abundant and crosses the placenta.','Agglutination means the antigen is present; type O has anti-A and anti-B; type AB has neither antibody.','Skill IV: label, 3 drops blood and 3 drops antiserum per well, separate stirrers 30 s; O-negative universal red cell donor; RhIG prevents HDFN.'],terms:[['Agglutination','Visible clumping when antibodies bind antigens on particles or cells.'],['Forward typing','Testing red cells with known antisera to detect antigens.'],['Crossmatch','Testing recipient plasma against donor red cells before transfusion.']],mcqs:[q('A person with type A blood has which antibodies in plasma?','Anti-B','Anti-A','Both anti-A and anti-B','Neither','Antibodies form against the antigen not present.'),q('Agglutination with anti-A and anti-D but not anti-B indicates:','A-positive','B-positive','O-negative','AB-positive','Agglutination means the antigen is present.'),q('The universal red cell donor type is:','O-negative','AB-positive','A-negative','B-positive','O-negative cells lack A, B and D antigens.'),q('In HOSA Skill IV, how many drops of blood go into each well?','Three','One','Five','Ten','Three drops of antiserum are also added.'),q('How long is each well stirred in Skill IV?','30 seconds','5 seconds','2 minutes','10 minutes','Separate clean stirrers are used.'),q('Which antibody class crosses the placenta?','IgG','IgM','IgA','IgE','This allows HDFN to occur.'),q('RhoGAM is given to prevent:','Hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn','Hemophilia','Iron deficiency','Leukemia','It prevents anti-D formation.'),q('A lateral flow pregnancy test is valid only when:','The control line appears','No lines appear','The test line is darker than the control','It is read after one hour','The control line confirms proper flow.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient needs a transfusion. Explain how you would determine the patient’s ABO group by slide testing following HOSA Skill IV, interpret a result of agglutination in the anti-B well only, and explain which red cells the patient could safely receive and why.',rubric:[['Skill IV procedure',['label','three drops','anti-a','anti-b','stir','30 seconds','separate']],['Interpretation of agglutination',['agglutination','antigen','type b','b antigen']],['Antibody reasoning',['anti-a','plasma','antibod']],['Compatible donors',['b','o','o-negative','compatible','crossmatch']]],model:'After hand hygiene and gloves, I would label a two-well slide with the patient’s name, place three drops of blood in each well without touching the dropper to the slide, add three drops of anti-A serum to the A well and three drops of anti-B to the B well, and stir each well with a separate clean stirrer for 30 seconds, then record agglutination. Agglutination only in the anti-B well means the red cells carry B antigen but not A antigen, so the patient is type B. A type B person has anti-A antibodies in plasma, so they must not receive red cells with A antigen. They can safely receive type B or type O red cells, matched for Rh status, after a crossmatch confirms compatibility; O-negative is reserved for emergencies.'},sources:[RULES,RC_BLOOD,NHLBI_TRANSFUSION]};

export const C7={title:'Urinalysis',hook:'Urine is easy to collect and full of clues: its color, clarity, concentration, chemistry and microscopic contents can point to dehydration, diabetes, kidney disease, liver disease or a urinary tract infection. A routine urinalysis has three parts, and HOSA’s Skill VI tests the first: the physical examination.',objectives:['Describe urine formation and specimen types including clean-catch and 24-hour collections.','Perform a physical examination of urine with a refractometer following HOSA Skill VI.','Interpret reagent strip chemical tests.','Recognize common findings in the microscopic examination.'],sections:[
['How urine forms and how it is collected',[
'Urine forms in the **nephrons** of the kidneys through **glomerular filtration**, **tubular reabsorption** and **tubular secretion**. Its composition reflects hydration, diet, metabolism and kidney function. Specimen type depends on the test. A **random** specimen is convenient for routine screening. A **first morning** specimen is most concentrated, useful for pregnancy tests and detecting abnormalities. A **clean-catch midstream** specimen, collected after cleansing and discarding the first portion, reduces contamination for **culture**. A **24-hour urine** collection, in a large container often with a preservative, measures total daily excretion of substances such as protein or creatinine; the patient discards the first morning void, collects every void for 24 hours and includes the next morning’s first void. Specimens should be tested within about 2 hours or refrigerated, because bacteria multiply, pH rises, glucose falls and cells break down at room temperature.',
match('Match the specimen type to its purpose',[['Most concentrated; pregnancy testing','First morning'],['Reduces contamination for culture','Clean-catch midstream'],['Measures total daily protein or creatinine','24-hour collection'],['Convenient routine screening','Random'],['Sterile specimen through a catheter port','Catheterized specimen']])]],
['Physical examination (HOSA Skill VI)',[
'Normal urine is **pale yellow to amber (straw)** from the pigment **urochrome** and is usually **clear**. Dark yellow suggests concentration; red or brown may indicate blood, hemoglobin, myoglobin or certain foods or drugs; amber-brown with yellow foam may indicate bilirubin. **Cloudy** urine may contain cells, bacteria, crystals or mucus. Odor is noted but has limited value; a fruity odor can suggest ketones. **Specific gravity (SG)** compares urine density with water and reflects concentrating ability; the typical range is about **1.005–1.030**.',
'In Skill VI, after assembling materials, hand hygiene and gloves with face protection, the competitor obtains the specimen and completes the demographic information on the laboratory form. They **mix the urine by gently swirling** and pour about **10 mL into a clear conical centrifuge tube**, then **observe and record color**, **observe and record transparency**, and **note odor** in the comments. To measure SG, they place **one drop of distilled water** on the refractometer’s glass plate, close the cover gently and read it: it should read **1.000**; if not, they **calibrate**. They clean the plate with a drop or two of water and dry it with laboratory tissue, add **one drop of well-mixed urine** with a transfer pipette, close the lid, read and **record the SG accurately**. Finally they clean the refractometer with lens paper and disinfectant, verbalize disposing of the specimen, disinfect the work area, dispose of PPE and perform hand hygiene.',
order('Physical examination of urine (HOSA Skill VI)',['Hand hygiene; gloves and face protection','Complete demographic information on the form','Swirl and pour about 10 mL into a clear conical tube','Record color and transparency; note odor','Check the refractometer reads 1.000 with distilled water','Clean the plate, add one drop of mixed urine and read SG','Record specific gravity','Clean the refractometer, dispose of specimen, disinfect and remove PPE'])]],
['Chemical examination with reagent strips',[
'A **reagent strip** (dipstick) has pads that change color; it is dipped briefly, excess removed by touching the edge to absorbent paper, and each pad read at the timed interval against the color chart or in a **strip reader**. Typical pads: **glucose** (glycosuria in uncontrolled diabetes), **ketones** (diabetic ketoacidosis, starvation; confirmed with an **Acetest** tablet), **protein** (kidney disease; mostly albumin), **blood** (hematuria, hemoglobinuria or myoglobinuria), **bilirubin** (liver or bile duct disease), **urobilinogen**, **pH** (normal about 4.5–8), **specific gravity**, **nitrite** (positive when certain bacteria convert nitrate, suggesting UTI) and **leukocyte esterase** (white cells, suggesting inflammation or infection). Strips are stored capped to protect from moisture and checked for expiration.',
sort('Match the strip result to a likely cause',['Diabetes-related','Infection-related','Kidney- or liver-related'],[['Positive glucose',0],['Positive ketones',0],['Positive nitrite',1],['Positive leukocyte esterase',1],['Positive protein',2],['Positive bilirubin',2]])]],
['Microscopic examination',[
'For the microscopic exam, about 10–12 mL of urine is **centrifuged**, the supernatant poured off and the **sediment** examined under low and high power. Findings include **red blood cells** (bleeding), **white blood cells** (inflammation; many WBCs with bacteria suggest UTI), **epithelial cells** (squamous cells suggest contamination of a poor clean-catch), **casts**—cylindrical structures formed in kidney tubules (hyaline casts can be normal; **RBC casts** suggest glomerulonephritis; **WBC casts** suggest pyelonephritis)—**crystals** (often normal, some linked to stones), **bacteria**, **yeast** and **parasites** such as *Trichomonas vaginalis*. Automated **urine particle analyzers** count elements in many laboratories, with manual review of abnormal results.',
{t:'w',kind:'label',diagram:'urinary',title:'Where urine forms and travels',parts:[['kidney','Kidney','Filters blood and forms urine in about a million nephrons each.'],['glomerulus','Glomerulus','Capillary tuft where filtration begins; damage lets protein and red cells into urine.'],['loop','Loop of Henle','Concentrates urine; influences specific gravity.'],['collecting','Collecting duct','Final adjustment of water and concentration.'],['ureter','Ureter','Carries urine from kidney to bladder.'],['bladder','Bladder','Stores urine; infection here causes cystitis.'],['urethra','Urethra','Carries urine out of the body; source of contamination in poor clean-catch collection.']]}]],
['Putting the findings together',[
kase('Interpret a urinalysis',{a:{text:'A cloudy, yellow specimen has SG 1.020, positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase, and many WBCs and bacteria in the sediment. What is most likely?',choices:[['Urinary tract infection','b','Correct. Nitrite, leukocyte esterase, WBCs and bacteria together suggest UTI.',true],['Diabetes mellitus','b','Glucose and ketones would be expected.',false]]},b:{text:'What additional specimen would confirm and guide treatment?',choices:[['A clean-catch midstream specimen for culture and susceptibility','c','Correct. Culture identifies the organism and effective antibiotics.',true],['A 24-hour urine collection','c','24-hour collections measure total excretion, not infection.',false]]},c:{text:'The culture grows a gram-negative rod, and susceptibility testing guides antibiotic choice.',end:true}}),
'Urinalysis results are interpreted together. Glucose with ketones suggests uncontrolled diabetes; protein with RBC casts suggests glomerular disease; bilirubin with dark urine suggests liver disease; high SG with dark color suggests dehydration. Errors to avoid: testing old specimens, not mixing before testing, reading strips at the wrong time and failing to calibrate the refractometer.']],
["Urine pregnancy and drug testing",["**Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)** is produced by the placenta and appears in urine and serum in early pregnancy. The **chromatographic immunoassay for urine hCG** on HOSA’s list is a lateral flow cassette: a few drops of urine are added to the sample well, and if hCG is present, labeled antibodies bind it and form a colored **test line**; a **control line** must always appear for the test to be valid. A first morning specimen is most concentrated and gives the best sensitivity early in pregnancy. Results are read at the time specified by the manufacturer; reading too late can show faint false lines.","Urine is also used for **drug screening** with immunoassays, followed by confirmatory testing for positives. Specimen **validity testing** checks temperature, specific gravity, pH and creatinine to detect diluted or adulterated samples, and **chain-of-custody** procedures document handling for workplace or legal testing."]]
],takeaways:['Use clean-catch for culture, first morning for concentration, 24-hour for total excretion; test within about 2 hours or refrigerate.','Skill VI: swirl, 10 mL in a clear tube, record color, clarity and odor; refractometer reads 1.000 with water before urine SG (normal about 1.005–1.030).','Strips detect glucose, ketones, protein, blood, bilirubin, nitrite and leukocyte esterase; casts form in kidney tubules.'],terms:[['Specific gravity','Density of urine compared with water.'],['Refractometer','Instrument measuring specific gravity by light refraction.'],['Cast','Cylindrical structure formed in kidney tubules and seen in sediment.']],mcqs:[q('What should a refractometer read with distilled water?','1.000','1.030','0.000','7.0','If not, calibrate it.'),q('Which specimen is best for urine culture?','Clean-catch midstream','Random unclean','24-hour','First morning without cleansing','It reduces contamination.'),q('A positive nitrite test suggests:','Bacterial urinary tract infection','Diabetes','Dehydration only','Liver disease','Certain bacteria convert nitrate to nitrite.'),q('Normal urine color comes mainly from:','Urochrome','Bilirubin','Hemoglobin','Glucose','Urochrome gives the yellow color.'),q('How much urine is poured into the conical tube in Skill VI?','About 10 mL','1 mL','50 mL','100 mL','It is used for observation.'),q('Which test confirms ketones?','Acetest tablet','Refractometer','hCG cassette','Gram stain','Acetest detects ketones.'),q('RBC casts in urine sediment suggest:','Glomerular disease','Normal urine','Contamination','Dehydration','Casts form in the tubules.'),q('Why must urine be tested within about 2 hours or refrigerated?','Bacteria multiply and components change at room temperature','It freezes','It becomes sterile','Specific gravity always drops to 1.000','Delays cause false results.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to perform the physical examination of urine following HOSA Skill VI, explain what normal results look like, and interpret a specimen that is cloudy with positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase.',rubric:[['Skill VI steps',['swirl','10 ml','color','transparency','odor','gloves']],['Refractometer use',['distilled water','1.000','calibrat','one drop','specific gravity']],['Normal values',['straw','yellow','clear','1.005','1.030']],['Interpretation',['urinary tract infection','uti','bacteria','culture','white']]],model:'After hand hygiene and putting on gloves and face protection, I would complete the patient information, gently swirl the urine and pour about 10 mL into a clear conical tube, then record its color and transparency and note any odor. I would check that the refractometer reads 1.000 with one drop of distilled water and calibrate it if not, clean and dry the plate, add one drop of well-mixed urine, close the lid, read the specific gravity and record it, then clean the instrument, dispose of the specimen, disinfect the area and remove PPE. Normal urine is pale yellow to amber, clear and has a specific gravity of about 1.005–1.030. A cloudy specimen with positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase suggests a bacterial urinary tract infection, which would be confirmed with a clean-catch culture and susceptibility testing.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_UA,NIDDK_KIDNEY]};

export const C8={title:'Clinical chemistry',hook:'A single tube of blood can be tested for dozens of substances—glucose, electrolytes, kidney waste products, liver enzymes, lipids and heart proteins. Clinical chemistry analyzers run hundreds of these tests an hour, and the laboratory professional ensures every number is accurate and interpreted against the right reference range.',objectives:['Explain spectrophotometry and how automated chemistry analyzers measure analytes.','Interpret glucose, HbA1c, electrolytes and kidney function tests.','Interpret lipid, liver and cardiac markers.','Describe specimen requirements, point-of-care testing and sources of error such as hemolysis.'],sections:[
['How chemistry tests are measured',[
'Most chemistry tests use **spectrophotometry**: a reaction produces a color, and the analyzer measures how much light the solution absorbs at a specific wavelength. Under **Beer’s law**, absorbance is proportional to concentration, so results are calculated from **calibrators** of known concentration. **Ion-selective electrodes** measure electrolytes such as sodium and potassium. **Immunoassays** use antibodies to measure hormones, drugs and cardiac markers. Chemistry tests are often run on **serum** (red or gold gel tube) or **plasma** (green heparin tube). Glucose that cannot be tested quickly is collected in a **gray-top tube** with **sodium fluoride**, which stops red cells from consuming glucose.',
match('Match the measurement method',[['Absorbance of colored reaction products','Spectrophotometry'],['Sodium and potassium measurement','Ion-selective electrode'],['Antibody-based measurement of hormones and troponin','Immunoassay'],['Known concentrations used to set up a test','Calibrators'],['Preservative that stops glycolysis in glucose tubes','Sodium fluoride']])]],
['Glucose and diabetes testing',[
'**Glucose** is the main blood sugar. A **fasting plasma glucose** of about **70–99 mg/dL** is normal; **100–125 mg/dL** indicates prediabetes; **126 mg/dL or higher** on two occasions indicates diabetes. **Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c)** reflects average glucose over about **2–3 months** because glucose attaches to hemoglobin during the red cells’ lifespan: below **5.7%** is normal, **5.7–6.4%** prediabetes and **6.5% or higher** diabetes. **Glucose meters** are waived point-of-care tests for monitoring, used with capillary blood from a **single-use lancet**; meters require quality control checks. Low glucose (**hypoglycemia**, often below 70 mg/dL) and very high glucose are critical values in many labs.',
sort('Normal, prediabetes or diabetes?',['Normal','Prediabetes','Diabetes'],[['Fasting glucose 88 mg/dL',0],['HbA1c 5.2%',0],['Fasting glucose 112 mg/dL',1],['HbA1c 6.0%',1],['Fasting glucose 140 mg/dL on two tests',2],['HbA1c 7.1%',2]])]],
['Electrolytes and kidney function',[
'A **basic metabolic panel (BMP)** includes glucose, calcium, **sodium** (about 135–145 mmol/L), **potassium** (about 3.5–5.0 mmol/L), **chloride**, **carbon dioxide (bicarbonate)**, **blood urea nitrogen (BUN)** and **creatinine**. Potassium is tightly regulated because both high and low levels cause dangerous heart rhythms. **BUN** and **creatinine** are waste products cleared by the kidneys; rising levels suggest reduced kidney function. The **estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR)**, calculated from creatinine, age and sex, stages chronic kidney disease. A **comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP)** adds liver tests and proteins.',
'Pre-analytical errors can mislead. **Hemolysis**—rupture of red cells from difficult draws, vigorous shaking or small needles—releases potassium and falsely raises it. Drawing above an IV line dilutes the sample. Prolonged tourniquet time can concentrate analytes. Recognizing a pink-red hemolyzed sample and requesting a recollection protects patients from unnecessary treatment.']],
['Lipids, liver and heart',[
'A **lipid panel** includes **total cholesterol** (desirable under 200 mg/dL), **LDL cholesterol** (“bad” cholesterol; lower is better), **HDL cholesterol** (“good” cholesterol; higher is protective) and **triglycerides**, traditionally measured after fasting 9–12 hours. **Liver function tests** include **ALT** and **AST** (enzymes released when liver cells are injured; ALT is more liver-specific), **alkaline phosphatase** (bile ducts and bone), **bilirubin** (from hemoglobin breakdown; high levels cause jaundice) and **albumin** (a protein made by the liver). **Cardiac troponin** is the preferred marker of heart muscle injury and rises within hours of a myocardial infarction; **BNP** helps assess heart failure. **Amylase** and **lipase** rise in pancreatitis.',
match('Match the analyte to what it suggests',[['High troponin','Heart muscle injury'],['High ALT','Liver cell injury'],['High bilirubin with jaundice','Liver or bile duct problem, or red cell breakdown'],['High lipase','Pancreatitis'],['High LDL','Increased atherosclerosis risk'],['Rising creatinine','Reduced kidney function']])]],
['Interpreting results responsibly',[
'Results are interpreted against **reference ranges** that depend on the method, population, age and sex. A value just outside the range is not always disease, and trends over time can matter more than one value. **Delta checks** flag large changes from a patient’s previous results, which may indicate a real change or a specimen error such as the wrong patient. **Critical values** are called immediately. The laboratory professional reviews quality control, instrument flags and specimen quality before releasing results.',
kase('A suspicious potassium',{a:{text:'A healthy outpatient’s potassium is 6.8 mmol/L. The serum looks pink-red. What should you suspect?',choices:[['Hemolysis falsely raising potassium','b','Correct. Ruptured red cells release potassium.',true],['A true emergency requiring immediate treatment without question','b','Specimen quality must be checked first.',false]]},b:{text:'What is the appropriate action?',choices:[['Note hemolysis and request a new specimen, notifying the provider','c','Correct. A recollection gives a reliable result.',true],['Report 6.8 without comment','c','Reporting a falsely high value could lead to harmful treatment.',false]]},c:{text:'The recollected, nonhemolyzed sample shows potassium 4.2 mmol/L, within the reference range.',end:true}})]],
["Point-of-care testing and quality",["**Point-of-care testing (POCT)** is performed near the patient—at the bedside, in clinics or at home—with devices such as **glucose meters**, **point-of-care coagulation analyzers**, hemoglobin meters, rapid strep tests and urine hCG cassettes. POCT gives fast results but is usually performed by nursing or clinic staff rather than laboratory professionals, so the laboratory often oversees training, competency assessment, quality control and documentation. Meters must be checked with **control solutions** at the required frequency, test strips must be within their expiration date and stored properly, and patient identification must be entered correctly.","Capillary samples from a **single-use lancet** should be collected after cleaning and drying the site, wiping away the first drop when the method requires, and avoiding excessive squeezing, which dilutes the sample with tissue fluid. Results outside the meter’s range or inconsistent with the patient’s condition are confirmed with a laboratory test on a venous sample."]]
],takeaways:['Spectrophotometry follows Beer’s law; electrolytes use ion-selective electrodes; gray-top sodium fluoride preserves glucose.','Fasting glucose ≥126 mg/dL or HbA1c ≥6.5% indicates diabetes; HbA1c reflects 2–3 months.','BMP: Na about 135–145, K about 3.5–5.0, BUN and creatinine for kidneys; troponin for MI; ALT for liver; hemolysis falsely raises potassium.'],terms:[['Spectrophotometry','Measuring light absorbance to determine concentration.'],['HbA1c','Glycated hemoglobin reflecting average glucose over 2–3 months.'],['Hemolysis','Rupture of red cells that can falsely raise potassium.']],mcqs:[q('Which test reflects average blood glucose over about 2–3 months?','HbA1c','Fasting glucose','Random glucose','Urine glucose','Glucose binds hemoglobin over the red cell lifespan.'),q('A fasting plasma glucose of 126 mg/dL or higher on two occasions indicates:','Diabetes','Normal glucose','Prediabetes','Hypoglycemia','126 is the diagnostic threshold.'),q('Which tube preserves glucose by stopping glycolysis?','Gray (sodium fluoride)','Lavender (EDTA)','Light blue (citrate)','Red (no additive)','Fluoride inhibits glycolysis.'),q('The preferred marker of heart muscle injury is:','Troponin','Amylase','ALT','Creatinine','Troponin rises after MI.'),q('Hemolysis most commonly falsely raises:','Potassium','Sodium','Glucose','HDL','Red cells contain much potassium.'),q('Which two tests assess kidney function in a BMP?','BUN and creatinine','ALT and AST','HDL and LDL','Amylase and lipase','Both are cleared by the kidneys.'),q('Beer’s law states absorbance is proportional to:','Concentration','Temperature','Time','Color only','This allows calculation from calibrators.'),q('Which liver enzyme is most specific for liver cell injury?','ALT','Alkaline phosphatase','Amylase','Troponin','AST is also found in muscle and heart.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient’s results show fasting glucose 150 mg/dL, HbA1c 7.4%, potassium 6.5 mmol/L in a pink-tinged sample, and normal troponin. Interpret each result and describe what the laboratory professional should do before releasing results.',rubric:[['Diabetes interpretation',['diabetes','126','6.5','2-3 months','2–3 months']],['Potassium and hemolysis',['hemoly','false','potassium','recollect']],['Troponin interpretation',['troponin','heart','normal','myocardial']],['Quality steps before release',['quality control','delta','critical','specimen','notify']]],model:'A fasting glucose of 150 mg/dL is above the 126 mg/dL diagnostic threshold and an HbA1c of 7.4% is above 6.5%, consistent with diabetes and showing elevated average glucose over the past two to three months. The potassium of 6.5 mmol/L in a pink-tinged sample suggests hemolysis, which releases potassium from red cells and falsely raises the result, so the specimen should be flagged and recollected rather than reported as a true critical value. A normal troponin does not suggest heart muscle injury. Before releasing results, the laboratory professional checks quality control and instrument flags, evaluates specimen quality, performs delta checks against previous results and calls any confirmed critical values with read-back.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_BMP,CDC_A1C,MEDLINE_LIVER]};

export const C9={title:'Clinical microbiology',hook:'A patient’s fever could come from hundreds of bacteria, and each responds differently to antibiotics. The microbiology laboratory grows the organism, identifies it and tests which antibiotics work—work that begins with a properly streaked plate, the skill HOSA tests in Skill VII.',objectives:['Collect and transport microbiology specimens correctly.','Perform and interpret the Gram stain and select appropriate culture media.','Streak an agar plate for isolated colonies following HOSA Skill VII.','Describe identification and antimicrobial susceptibility testing and the threat of resistance.'],sections:[
['Specimens and transport',[
'Microbiology results are only as good as the specimen. Collect **before antibiotics** when possible, from the actual site of infection, with **sterile** technique and enough volume. Common specimens include blood cultures (drawn in sets from separate sites, first in the order of draw), urine (clean-catch or catheter), throat and wound swabs, sputum (deep cough, not saliva), stool and cerebrospinal fluid. **Culture swabs and transport tubes** contain media that keep organisms alive during transport. Label every specimen with patient identifiers, source, date and time, and deliver promptly; some specimens must be kept at room temperature (cerebrospinal fluid) and others refrigerated (urine if delayed).',
match('Match the specimen practice',[['Collect before this whenever possible','Antibiotic therapy'],['Drawn in sets from separate sites','Blood cultures'],['Should come from a deep cough, not saliva','Sputum'],['Keeps organisms alive on swabs','Transport medium'],['Must include source, date and time','Specimen label']])]],
['The Gram stain',[
'The **Gram stain** divides bacteria by cell wall structure and gives rapid clues. Steps: (1) **crystal violet**, the primary stain, colors all cells purple; (2) **Gram’s iodine**, the **mordant**, forms a complex that traps the dye; (3) **decolorizer** (alcohol or acetone-alcohol) washes the dye out of **Gram-negative** cells, whose thin peptidoglycan and outer membrane cannot hold it; (4) **safranin**, the **counterstain**, colors the decolorized cells pink-red. **Gram-positive** bacteria (thick peptidoglycan) stay **purple**; **Gram-negative** bacteria stain **pink**. Shape and arrangement add information: **cocci in clusters** suggest *Staphylococcus*; **cocci in chains**, *Streptococcus*; **gram-negative rods**, organisms such as *E. coli*. Over-decolorizing makes gram-positive cells look pink—the most common error.',
order('Order the Gram stain steps',['Heat- or methanol-fix the smear','Crystal violet (primary stain)','Gram’s iodine (mordant)','Alcohol decolorizer','Safranin (counterstain)','Rinse, blot dry and examine under oil immersion'])]],
['Culture media',[
'Media support or select organisms. **Nonselective, enriched** media such as **blood agar (sheep blood agar)** grow most bacteria and show **hemolysis**: **beta** (complete clearing, as with *Streptococcus pyogenes*), **alpha** (partial, greenish) and **gamma** (none). **Chocolate agar** (heated blood) supports **fastidious** organisms such as *Haemophilus* and *Neisseria*, often incubated with extra carbon dioxide in a CO₂ incubator or **candle jar**. **MacConkey agar** is **selective** for gram-negative bacteria and **differential** for **lactose fermentation**: lactose fermenters such as *E. coli* form pink colonies. **Thayer-Martin** agar selects for *Neisseria gonorrhoeae*. Plates are incubated at **35–37 °C**, usually upside down, and examined for colony size, color, shape and hemolysis.',
sort('Which medium?',['Blood agar','Chocolate agar','MacConkey agar'],[['Shows beta, alpha or gamma hemolysis',0],['General enriched medium for most bacteria',0],['Grows fastidious Haemophilus and Neisseria',1],['Heated blood releases nutrients',1],['Selects gram-negative bacteria',2],['Lactose fermenters appear pink',2]])]],
['Streaking a plate (HOSA Skill VII)',[
'In Skill VII, the competitor assembles materials, uses alcohol-based handrub and puts on **gloves and face protection**, selects an agar plate and **labels the bottom with a marker**. They remove a **pre-inoculated swab**, open the plate lid **just enough** to insert it, **spread the inoculum over one quadrant**, replace the lid and discard the swab in the biohazard receptacle. With a **sterile disposable loop**, lifting the lid only enough to insert it, they touch the first quadrant and **streak across the second quadrant with six to eight strokes**, then discard the loop. With a **new sterile loop**, they touch the second quadrant and streak the **third quadrant with six to eight strokes**, then discard the loop. With a third sterile loop, they touch the third quadrant and spread into the **fourth quadrant with a continuous “tornado” streak**, **decreasing the width of streaks and increasing the distance between them**, to produce isolated colonies. They replace the lid, discard the loop and place the plate **upside down in the 35–37 °C incubator**.',
order('Streak for isolated colonies (HOSA Skill VII)',['Hand hygiene, gloves and face protection','Label the bottom of the plate','Spread the swab over quadrant 1 and discard the swab','New sterile loop: from quadrant 1, streak quadrant 2 with 6–8 strokes','New sterile loop: from quadrant 2, streak quadrant 3 with 6–8 strokes','New sterile loop: from quadrant 3, tornado streak into quadrant 4','Replace the lid and discard the loop','Incubate upside down at 35–37 °C'])]],
['Identification and susceptibility',[
'Isolated colonies are identified using Gram stain, colony appearance, **biochemical tests** (for example, **catalase** separates staphylococci, which bubble, from streptococci; **coagulase** identifies *Staphylococcus aureus*), automated identification systems and **MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry** in many laboratories. **Antimicrobial susceptibility testing** shows which antibiotics inhibit the organism. In the **Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion** test, antibiotic disks on a lawn of bacteria create **zones of inhibition**; zone diameters are interpreted as susceptible, intermediate or resistant. Broth dilution methods report the **minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC)**, the lowest concentration preventing visible growth.',
'**Antimicrobial resistance** is a major public health threat. The CDC tracks organisms such as **MRSA** (methicillin-resistant *S. aureus*), **VRE** (vancomycin-resistant enterococci), **carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (CRE)** and drug-resistant *C. difficile*. Laboratories help through accurate testing, reporting and supporting antibiotic stewardship.',
kase('From culture to treatment',{a:{text:'A wound culture grows gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase positive and coagulase positive. What organism is most likely?',choices:[['Staphylococcus aureus','b','Correct. Catalase and coagulase positive gram-positive cocci in clusters indicate S. aureus.',true],['Streptococcus pyogenes','b','Streptococci grow in chains and are catalase negative.',false]]},b:{text:'Susceptibility testing shows resistance to oxacillin (methicillin). What does this mean?',choices:[['The isolate is MRSA, requiring alternative antibiotics and contact precautions','c','Correct. MRSA needs different treatment and infection control.',true],['The isolate is susceptible to all penicillins','c','Oxacillin resistance indicates resistance to beta-lactams.',false]]},c:{text:'The laboratory reports MRSA, the care team adjusts therapy and contact precautions are started.',end:true}})]],
["Fungi, viruses and acid-fast bacteria",["Microbiology also covers fungi and viruses. **Yeasts** such as *Candida albicans* cause thrush, vaginal and bloodstream infections; **molds** such as *Aspergillus* grow as filaments and threaten immunocompromised patients. A **potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation** dissolves skin and hair cells so fungal elements can be seen under the microscope. Fungal cultures grow more slowly on media such as Sabouraud dextrose agar. Viruses cannot grow on agar; they are detected mainly by **molecular tests** (PCR panels), **antigen tests** and **serology** for antibodies.","**Mycobacteria**, including *Mycobacterium tuberculosis*, have waxy cell walls that resist the Gram stain. The **acid-fast stain** (Ziehl-Neelsen or Kinyoun) uses carbolfuchsin, an acid-alcohol decolorizer and methylene blue counterstain; acid-fast bacilli appear **red** against a blue background. TB cultures can take weeks, so molecular tests help speed diagnosis, and specimens are handled in a biological safety cabinet with respiratory protection."]]
],takeaways:['Collect before antibiotics, sterilely, from the infection site; label source, date and time.','Gram stain: crystal violet, iodine, decolorizer, safranin; gram-positive purple, gram-negative pink.','Skill VII: label bottom, swab quadrant 1, new loop each quadrant with 6–8 strokes, tornado in quadrant 4, incubate upside down at 35–37 °C.'],terms:[['Mordant','Gram’s iodine, which traps crystal violet in the cell.'],['Selective medium','Medium that inhibits some organisms while allowing others to grow.'],['Zone of inhibition','Clear area around an antibiotic disk where bacteria did not grow.']],mcqs:[q('In the Gram stain, the mordant is:','Gram’s iodine','Crystal violet','Safranin','Alcohol','It traps the primary stain.'),q('Gram-negative bacteria appear:','Pink','Purple','Green','Black','They lose crystal violet and take up safranin.'),q('MacConkey agar selects for:','Gram-negative bacteria','Gram-positive bacteria','Fungi only','Viruses','Lactose fermenters turn pink.'),q('Which medium supports fastidious organisms like Haemophilus?','Chocolate agar','MacConkey agar','Blood agar only','Saline','Heated blood releases nutrients.'),q('In HOSA Skill VII, how many strokes are made in quadrants 2 and 3?','Six to eight','One','Twenty','Fifty','A new sterile loop is used for each quadrant.'),q('At what temperature is the plate incubated in Skill VII?','35–37 °C','4 °C','25 °C','56 °C','Body temperature favors human pathogens.'),q('A catalase-positive, coagulase-positive gram-positive coccus in clusters is most likely:','Staphylococcus aureus','Streptococcus pyogenes','E. coli','Neisseria','Coagulase identifies S. aureus.'),q('The Kirby-Bauer test measures:','Zones of inhibition around antibiotic disks','Blood glucose','Specific gravity','Antibody titers','Zone size indicates susceptibility.')],frq:{prompt:'A swab from an infected wound arrives in the laboratory. Describe how you would streak it for isolated colonies following HOSA Skill VII, perform and interpret a Gram stain, and determine which antibiotics would work.',rubric:[['Skill VII streaking',['label','quadrant','sterile loop','6','8','tornado','upside down']],['Gram stain steps and interpretation',['crystal violet','iodine','decolor','safranin','purple','pink']],['Media and identification',['blood agar','macconkey','catalase','coagulase','colon']],['Susceptibility testing',['kirby-bauer','disk','zone','mic','resistant']]],model:'After hand hygiene, gloves and face protection, I would label the bottom of the plate, spread the swab over the first quadrant and discard it, then use a new sterile loop for each quadrant: from quadrant one I would streak quadrant two with six to eight strokes, from two into three with six to eight strokes, and from three into four with a tornado pattern of widening spaces, then incubate the plate upside down at 35–37 °C. For the Gram stain I would apply crystal violet, Gram’s iodine, an alcohol decolorizer and safranin; gram-positive bacteria stay purple and gram-negative bacteria stain pink, and shape and arrangement such as cocci in clusters give clues. I would identify colonies using growth on blood and MacConkey agar and tests such as catalase and coagulase. Finally, Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion or MIC testing would show which antibiotics are susceptible, intermediate or resistant.'},sources:[RULES,ASM_GRAM,CDC_AR]};

export const C10={title:'Parasitology',hook:'A child scratches at night, a traveler returns with recurring fevers, and a hiker develops diarrhea after drinking from a stream. All three could have parasites, and each requires a different specimen and test—from a sticky paddle pressed to the skin at dawn to a stained blood smear examined drop by drop.',objectives:['Classify medically important parasites as protozoa, helminths or ectoparasites.','Collect specimens for ova and parasite examination and pinworm testing.','Describe blood parasite diagnosis, especially malaria smears.','Explain life cycles, transmission and prevention of common parasites.'],sections:[
['Types of parasites',[
'A **parasite** lives on or in a **host** and benefits at the host’s expense. Medical parasitology covers three groups. **Protozoa** are single-celled eukaryotes, including **intestinal protozoa** such as *Giardia duodenalis* and *Entamoeba histolytica*, **blood and tissue protozoa** such as *Plasmodium* (malaria) and *Toxoplasma gondii*, and *Trichomonas vaginalis* (a sexually transmitted protozoan). **Helminths** are multicellular worms: **nematodes** (roundworms such as *Enterobius vermicularis*, the pinworm, and *Ascaris lumbricoides*), **cestodes** (tapeworms such as *Taenia*) and **trematodes** (flukes such as *Schistosoma*). **Ectoparasites** live on the surface, such as lice, scabies mites and ticks. Many parasites have **life cycles** with stages in different hosts: the **definitive host** harbors the adult or sexual stage, and an **intermediate host** harbors larval or asexual stages. **Vectors** such as mosquitoes transmit some parasites.',
sort('Classify each parasite',['Protozoa','Nematodes (roundworms)','Cestodes (tapeworms)','Trematodes (flukes)'],[['Giardia duodenalis',0],['Plasmodium falciparum',0],['Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm)',1],['Ascaris lumbricoides',1],['Taenia solium',2],['Schistosoma mansoni',3]])]],
['Stool specimens and the O&P exam',[
'The **ova and parasite (O&P) examination** looks for eggs (ova), larvae, cysts and trophozoites in stool. Specimens are collected in a clean **stool specimen container**, avoiding contamination with urine or water, and placed in **preservatives** such as **10% formalin** (for concentration and wet mounts) and **polyvinyl alcohol (PVA)** or other fixatives (for permanent stained smears) when they cannot be examined quickly. Because parasites are shed intermittently, a common recommendation is **three specimens collected on alternate days**. Laboratory steps include a **direct wet mount**, a **concentration procedure** (formalin–ethyl acetate sedimentation) and a **permanent stain** such as trichrome. Antigen tests and molecular panels now detect *Giardia*, *Cryptosporidium* and others rapidly. Protozoa are seen as **cysts** (resistant, infective stage passed in formed stool) and **trophozoites** (active feeding stage, often in liquid stool).',
order('Steps in an O&P examination',['Collect stool in a clean container without urine or water','Add specimen to formalin and PVA preservatives if not tested quickly','Prepare a direct wet mount','Concentrate by formalin–ethyl acetate sedimentation','Prepare and examine a permanent stained smear','Report organisms and stages found'])]],
['Pinworm testing',[
'**Pinworm** (*Enterobius vermicularis*) is the most common worm infection in the United States, especially in school-age children. Female worms migrate out at night to lay eggs around the anus, causing itching; eggs spread by scratching, contaminated hands, bedding and surfaces. Eggs are rarely found in stool, so testing uses the **perianal paddle** (on HOSA’s instrument list) or **cellulose tape** method: the sticky surface is pressed against the perianal skin **first thing in the morning, before bathing or using the toilet**, then examined under the microscope for the flattened, oval eggs. Testing on several consecutive mornings increases sensitivity. Treatment usually includes household members, along with hand washing, trimmed fingernails and laundering bedding.',
{t:'check',q:'When should a perianal paddle specimen for pinworm be collected?',options:['First thing in the morning before bathing or toileting','After a shower in the evening','Any time from a stool sample','After lunch'],why:'Females lay eggs overnight; bathing removes them.'}]],
['Blood parasites and malaria',[
'**Malaria** is caused by *Plasmodium* species (*P. falciparum*, the most dangerous, plus *P. vivax*, *P. ovale*, *P. malariae* and *P. knowlesi*) transmitted by female ***Anopheles*** mosquitoes. Parasites multiply in the liver, then invade red cells, causing cycles of fever, chills and anemia. Malaria is a medical emergency in a returning traveler with fever. Diagnosis uses **thick and thin blood smears** stained with **Giemsa**: the **thick smear** concentrates blood to **detect** parasites, and the **thin smear** allows **species identification** and calculation of **parasitemia** (percentage of infected red cells). Smears are repeated every 12–24 hours for a total of three sets if initial smears are negative but suspicion remains. **Rapid diagnostic tests** detect malaria antigens, and PCR confirms species. Other blood parasites include *Babesia* (tick-borne) and *Trypanosoma*.',
match('Match the malaria term',[['Vector that transmits malaria','Female Anopheles mosquito'],['Most dangerous malaria species','Plasmodium falciparum'],['Smear used to detect parasites','Thick smear'],['Smear used to identify species','Thin smear'],['Stain used for malaria smears','Giemsa'],['Percentage of infected red cells','Parasitemia']])]],
['Prevention and public health',[
'Prevention breaks transmission: **safe water** and filtering or boiling water outdoors (*Giardia* and *Cryptosporidium*), **thorough cooking** of meat (*Taenia* and *Toxoplasma*), **hand hygiene** and sanitation (pinworm and many intestinal parasites), avoiding **contact with contaminated freshwater** (*Schistosoma*), **insect repellents, bed nets and antimalarial prophylaxis** for travelers (malaria) and **avoiding cat litter during pregnancy** (*Toxoplasma*). Laboratory professionals protect themselves by treating all specimens with standard precautions, because some parasites, such as *Cryptosporidium* oocysts, are infectious when passed. The CDC’s **DPDx** resource provides images and identification guidance used in training and practice.',
kase('A returning traveler',{a:{text:'A student returns from a trip to sub-Saharan Africa with fever and chills every two days. What test is most urgent?',choices:[['Thick and thin blood smears (and a rapid malaria antigen test)','b','Correct. Malaria must be ruled out quickly.',true],['A stool O&P only','b','Fever with travel to a malaria region requires blood smears.',false]]},b:{text:'The thin smear shows many ring forms, with several red cells containing multiple rings. What does this suggest?',choices:[['Plasmodium falciparum with significant parasitemia','c','Correct. Multiple rings per cell and high parasitemia suggest falciparum.',true],['Pinworm infection','c','Pinworm is an intestinal nematode, not a blood parasite.',false]]},c:{text:'The laboratory reports P. falciparum with a calculated parasitemia, and the patient begins urgent treatment.',end:true}})]],
["Ectoparasites and tissue parasites",["**Ectoparasites** live on the skin or hair. **Head lice** (*Pediculus humanus capitis*) attach eggs called **nits** to hair shafts near the scalp; **scabies** mites (*Sarcoptes scabiei*) burrow into skin, causing intense itching, and are confirmed by skin scrapings examined under the microscope. **Ticks** are not only parasites themselves but vectors of bacterial and protozoal diseases, including Lyme disease (bacterial) and babesiosis (protozoal). Identifying arthropods brought to the laboratory helps clinicians assess disease risk.","Some protozoa infect tissues. *Toxoplasma gondii* is acquired from undercooked meat or cat feces and can harm a developing fetus, so it is diagnosed mainly by serology. *Trichomonas vaginalis* is identified by wet mount, where motile, pear-shaped trophozoites with flagella are seen, or by molecular tests. *Cryptosporidium* causes waterborne diarrhea and is detected by modified acid-fast stains or antigen tests. Across all parasites, accurate specimen collection and timely examination determine whether the organism is found."]]
],takeaways:['Protozoa are single-celled; helminths include nematodes, cestodes and trematodes; ectoparasites live on the surface.','O&P: three stool specimens on alternate days in formalin and PVA; wet mount, concentration and permanent stain.','Pinworm: perianal paddle first thing in the morning; malaria: Giemsa thick (detect) and thin (identify, parasitemia) smears.'],terms:[['Ova and parasite exam','Microscopic examination of stool for parasite eggs, cysts and trophozoites.'],['Trophozoite','Active, feeding stage of a protozoan.'],['Definitive host','Host in which a parasite reaches its adult or sexual stage.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a protozoan parasite?','Giardia duodenalis','Enterobius vermicularis','Taenia solium','Schistosoma mansoni','Protozoa are single-celled.'),q('The perianal paddle test detects:','Pinworm eggs','Malaria parasites','Giardia cysts in stool','Tapeworm segments in blood','It collects eggs from perianal skin.'),q('When should a pinworm specimen be collected?','First thing in the morning before bathing','After an evening bath','From blood','At noon','Eggs are laid overnight.'),q('Which mosquito transmits malaria?','Female Anopheles','Aedes','Culex','Male Anopheles','Only female Anopheles transmit malaria.'),q('The thick blood smear is used to:','Detect parasites','Identify species precisely','Measure hemoglobin','Count platelets','The thin smear identifies species.'),q('How many stool specimens are commonly recommended for O&P testing?','Three on alternate days','One only','Ten consecutive days','Two on the same day','Parasites are shed intermittently.'),q('Tapeworms are classified as:','Cestodes','Nematodes','Trematodes','Protozoa','Flukes are trematodes.'),q('Which stain is used for malaria smears?','Giemsa','Gram','Acid-fast','Trichrome','Giemsa shows Plasmodium stages.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare how the laboratory would test for pinworm, intestinal protozoa and malaria, including specimen collection, laboratory methods and prevention advice for each.',rubric:[['Pinworm testing',['perianal','paddle','tape','morning','before bathing']],['Intestinal protozoa O&P',['stool','three','formalin','pva','wet mount','concentrat']],['Malaria testing',['thick','thin','giemsa','parasitemia','anopheles']],['Prevention advice',['hand','water','bed net','repellent','cook']]],model:'For pinworm, a perianal paddle or cellulose tape is pressed to the perianal skin first thing in the morning before bathing or toileting and examined for eggs, repeated on several mornings. For intestinal protozoa such as Giardia, three stool specimens on alternate days are collected in clean containers with formalin and PVA preservatives and examined by wet mount, formalin–ethyl acetate concentration and a permanent stain, or by antigen tests. For malaria, Giemsa-stained thick smears detect parasites and thin smears identify the Plasmodium species and parasitemia, repeated every 12–24 hours if suspicion remains, supported by rapid antigen tests. Prevention includes hand washing and laundering bedding for pinworm, safe or boiled water for Giardia, and insect repellent, bed nets and antimalarial medicine for travelers to malaria regions.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_DPDX,CDC_PINWORM,CDC_MALARIA]};
